        {q:'A witness identifies a suspect from a single photo shown by an investigator. What is the main evidential weakness?',opts:['Photos are never evidence','The procedure may be suggestive and needs an independently fair identification process','The witness must be lying','The suspect is automatically guilty'],a:1,why:'A single-photo presentation can cue a witness; use a fair, documented procedure and corroboration.'},
        {q:'A file hash differs after transfer. What should an analyst check first?',opts:['Delete the original','Compare trusted source hash, transfer logs and acquisition method','Assume deliberate tampering','Rename the file'],a:1,why:'A mismatch can arise from transfer or acquisition errors as well as tampering.'},
        {q:'Two independent witnesses give matching times, but both relied on the same group chat message. How should their accounts be treated?',opts:['As fully independent corroboration','As potentially dependent accounts with a shared source','As proof of the event','As automatically false'],a:1,why:'Shared information means the accounts may not be independent.'},
        {q:'A document has a valid signature but an altered timestamp in its metadata. What does that establish?',opts:['The signer altered it','The metadata differs and needs provenance analysis','The entire document is false','Nothing can be investigated'],a:1,why:'Metadata is a lead; determine how it was generated and whether the signed content changed.'}
